.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt SSL_CTX_SET_SESSION_TICKET_CB 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata ,
.Nm SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_generate_session_ticket_fn ,
.Nm SSL_CTX_decrypt_session_ticket_fn
.Nd manage session ticket application data
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/ssl.h
.Ft typedef int
.Fo 
.Fa *SSL_CTX_generate_session_ticket_fn
.Fc
(SSL *s, void *arg);
.Ft typedef SSL_TICKET_RETURN
.Fo 
.Fa *SSL_CTX_decrypt_session_ticket_fn
.Fc
(SSL *s, SSL_SESSION *ss,
.Bd -literal
                                                                const unsigned char *keyname,
                                                                size_t keyname_len,
                                                                SSL_TICKET_STATUS status,
                                                                void *arg);
 int SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb(SSL_CTX *ctx,
                                   SSL_CTX_generate_session_ticket_fn gen_cb,
                                   SSL_CTX_decrypt_session_ticket_fn dec_cb,
                                   void *arg);
 int SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata(SSL_SESSION *ss, const void *data, size_t len);
 int SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata(SSL_SESSION *ss, void **data, size_t *len);
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_set_session_ticket_cb 3
sets the application callbacks
.Sy gen_cb
and
.Sy dec_cb
that are used by a server to set and get application data stored with a
session, and placed into a session ticket.
Either callback function may be set to NULL. The value of
.Sy arg
is passed to the callbacks.
.Pp
.Sy gen_cb
is the application defined callback invoked when a session ticket is
about to be created.
The application can call
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3
at this time to add application data to the session ticket.
The value of
.Sy arg
is the same as that given to
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb 3 .
The
.Sy gen_cb
callback is defined as type
.Fa SSL_CTX_generate_session_ticket_fn .
.Pp
.Sy dec_cb
is the application defined callback invoked after session ticket
decryption has been attempted and any session ticket application data is
available.
If ticket decryption was successful then the
.Sy ss
argument contains the session data.
The
.Sy keyname
and
.Sy keyname_len
arguments identify the key used to decrypt the session ticket.
The
.Sy status
argument is the result of the ticket decryption.
See the
.Sx NOTES
section below for further details.
The value of
.Sy arg
is the same as that given to
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb 3 .
The
.Sy dec_cb
callback is defined as type
.Fa SSL_CTX_decrypt_session_ticket_fn .
.Pp
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3
sets the application data specified by
.Sy data
and
.Sy len
into
.Sy ss
which is then placed into any generated session tickets.
It can be called at any time before a session ticket is created to
update the data placed into the session ticket.
However, given that sessions and tickets are created by the handshake,
the
.Sy gen_cb
is provided to notify the application that a session ticket is about to
be generated.
.Pp
.Xr SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata 3
assigns
.Sy data
to the session ticket application data and assigns
.Sy len
to the length of the session ticket application data from
.Sy ss .
The application data can be set via
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3
or by a session ticket.
NULL will be assigned to
.Sy data
and 0 will be assigned to
.Sy len
if there is no session ticket application data.
.Xr SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata 3
can be called any time after a session has been created.
The
.Sy dec_cb
is provided to notify the application that a session ticket has just
been decrypted.
.Sh NOTES
When the
.Sy dec_cb
callback is invoked, the SSL_SESSION
.Sy ss
has not yet been assigned to the SSL
.Sy s .
The
.Sy status
indicates the result of the ticket decryption.
The callback must check the
.Sy status
value before performing any action, as it is called even if ticket
decryption fails.
.Pp
The
.Sy keyname
and
.Sy keyname_len
arguments to
.Sy dec_cb
may be used to identify the key that was used to encrypt the session
ticket.
.Pp
The
.Sy status
argument can be any of these values:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It SSL_TICKET_EMPTY
Empty ticket present.
No ticket data will be used and a new ticket should be sent to the
client.
This only occurs in TLSv1.2 or below.
In TLSv1.3 it is not valid for a client to send an empty ticket.
.It SSL_TICKET_NO_DECRYPT
The ticket couldn't be decrypted.
No ticket data will be used and a new ticket should be sent to the
client.
.It SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS
A ticket was successfully decrypted, any session ticket application data
should be available.
A new ticket should not be sent to the client.
.It SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS_RENEW
Same as
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS ,
but a new ticket should be sent to the client.
.El
.Pp
The return value can be any of these values:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It SSL_TICKET_RETURN_ABORT
The handshake should be aborted, either because of an error or because
of some policy.
Note that in TLSv1.3 a client may send more than one ticket in a single
handshake.
Therefore, just because one ticket is unacceptable it does not mean that
all of them are.
For this reason this option should be used with caution.
.It SSL_TICKET_RETURN_IGNORE
Do not use a ticket (if one was available).
Do not send a renewed ticket to the client.
.It SSL_TICKET_RETURN_IGNORE_RENEW
Do not use a ticket (if one was available).
Send a renewed ticket to the client.
.Pp
If the callback does not wish to change the default ticket behaviour
then it should return this value if
.Sy status
is
.Sy SSL_TICKET_EMPTY
or
.Sy SSL_TICKET_NO_DECRYPT .
.It SSL_TICKET_RETURN_USE
Use the ticket.
Do not send a renewed ticket to the client.
It is an error for the callback to return this value if
.Sy status
has a value other than
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS
or
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS_RENEW .
.Pp
If the callback does not wish to change the default ticket behaviour
then it should return this value if
.Sy status
is
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS .
.It SSL_TICKET_RETURN_USE_RENEW
Use the ticket.
Send a renewed ticket to the client.
It is an error for the callback to return this value if
.Sy status
has a value other than
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS
or
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS_RENEW .
.Pp
If the callback does not wish to change the default ticket behaviour
then it should return this value if
.Sy status
is
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS_RENEW .
.El
.Pp
If
.Sy status
has the value
.Sy SSL_TICKET_EMPTY
or
.Sy SSL_TICKET_NO_DECRYPT
then no session data will be available and the callback must not use the
.Sy ss
argument.
If
.Sy status
has the value
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS
or
.Sy SSL_TICKET_SUCCESS_RENEW
then the application can call
.Xr SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata 3
using the session provided in the
.Sy ss
argument to retrieve the application data.
.Pp
When the
.Sy gen_cb
callback is invoked, the
.Xr SSL_get_session 3
function can be used to retrieve the SSL_SESSION for
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3 .
.Pp
By default, in TLSv1.2 and below, a new session ticket is not issued on
a successful resumption and therefore
.Sy gen_cb
will not be called.
In TLSv1.3 the default behaviour is to always issue a new ticket on
resumption.
In both cases this behaviour can be changed if a ticket key callback is
in use (see
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_tlsext_ticket_key_cb 3 Ns ).
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb 3 ,
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3
and
.Xr SSL_SESSION_get0_ticket_appdata 3
functions return 1 on success and 0 on failure.
.Pp
The
.Sy gen_cb
callback must return 1 to continue the connection.
A return of 0 will terminate the connection with an INTERNAL_ERROR
alert.
.Pp
The
.Sy dec_cb
callback must return a value as described in
.Sx NOTES
above.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ssl 7 ,
.Xr SSL_get_session 3
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Xr SSL_CTX_set_session_ticket_cb 3 ,
.Xr SSL_SESSION_set1_ticket_appdata 3
and
.Xr SSL_SESSION_get_ticket_appdata 3
functions were added in OpenSSL 1.1.1.
